WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:07.180
A ton has been made of a recent report that
says, Oh, oh, a $15 minimum wage would destroy

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:13.170
more than 1 million jobs. And this report
is being cited by right-wingers all over the

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:20.410
country as proof proof that a $15 minimum
wage would be terrible for the country and

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:24.520
we shouldn't do it. And in fact, this is being
used to argue that we shouldn't even have

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.289
a minimum wage at all because it's an artificial
price floor and it's bad for the economy.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:34.589
So I want to actually talk this through with
you today because you might be shocked to

00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:40.210
hear this, but the right wing reaction to
this study makes no sense at all and is patently

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:45.600
dishonest. So let's start with what the study
says. Right now, the federal minimum wage

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.809
is $7 and 25 cents an hour, which is insane.
I often forget about this because I live in

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:55.609
Massachusetts, but federally, even though
many states have higher minimum wages federally,

00:00:55.609 --> 00:01:02.350
the minimum wage is $7 and 25 cents an hour.
The study by the CBO looked at increases to

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:08.610
$10 an hour, $12 an hour, and $15 an hour
by 2025 we're just going to focus on the 15

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:15.560
an hour to keep it simple. Today, increasing
the minimum wage to $15 by 2025 the CBO study

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:21.800
found would increase the effective net wages
of 17 million people who would otherwise be

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.119
earning under $15 an hour.

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:28.719
That's good news. In addition to raising the
wages of 17 million, who would be earning

00:01:28.719 --> 00:01:34.450
under $15 an hour, it would also push up slightly
the wages of another 10 million people who

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:41.830
are earning right around 15 or we'll be earning
right around $15 an hour in 2025 so 27 million

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:47.229
people in total will have higher wages as
a result of this. A minimum wage increase.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:54.560
However, about 1.3 million people would be
moved a a would would not have a job who otherwise

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.540
would people, it doesn't necessarily mean
people will be fired, but it means that in

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.979
2025 with a $15 minimum wage, there are 1.3
million people who would otherwise have jobs

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.820
who will not because of that minimum wage
increase and it would have a slightly inflationary

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:16.060
effect on prices overall. So first of all,
we have to understand that these numbers,

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:22.909
the CBO admits are uncertain because the CBO
straight up admits, we don't know how responsive

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:27.129
unemployment or employment are to changes
in the minimum wage.

00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:32.720
They admit there is a lot of uncertainty about
how sensitive is employment relative to a

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.390
change in the minimum wage. Is it extremely
sensitive or is it only slightly sensitive?

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:41.819
In addition, there are other studies published
at the exact same time that say there would

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:48.530
not be this effect of 1.3 million jobs that
would not exist were it not for that $15 minimum

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:55.069
wage, but for this discussion, let's imagine
that these numbers are 100% accurate from

00:02:55.069 --> 00:03:00.980
the CBO. The question becomes does it make
sense to increase the minimum wage to $15

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:08.950
or not? So the most childish argument is any
policy that leads to any job loss is a bad

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.799
policy. Now this is an outrageous position
to take because almost any economic policy

00:03:13.799 --> 00:03:18.470
decision has some gains in some losses and
you basically can't do anything. I mean, the

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:24.550
government would not be able to do any economic
policy if the rule was, if any jobs are lost,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:25.550
we don't do it.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:30.000
So that's not even a serious argument. It
is true that to come in and in some states

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.659
you would be effectively more than doubling
the minimum wage. That is going to affect

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:41.079
employers more if the wage than if the wage
had been increasing with inflation all along.

00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:46.909
And that's part of the problem here. We are
so far behind on minimum wage increases in

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:52.579
states that still have a minimum wage that
is equal to the federal minimum wage of seven

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:58.060
25 that we now have a situation, we're going
to $15 would more than double the minimum

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.040
wage in some states. So this is how you have
an adult conversation. There's a benefit to

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:10.109
the policy, but it also comes with a cost.
Is the cost too high? Is the cost acceptable,

00:04:10.109 --> 00:04:15.900
is it low? And we get a lot more benefit than
we lose by putting in place this policy.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.600
When you look at where a $15 minimum wage
would have the biggest impact, you immediately

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.100
see that the gains will be in the places where
people much more desperately needed in terms

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:33.730
of wage growth and the job losses will also
be disproportionately in the places that are

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:39.200
so far behind on the minimum wage right now
that going to $15 would be effectively doubling

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.040
the minimum wage in their state. So can I
answer for you whether it makes sense in your

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:51.450
state to have this tradeoff? I can't really
do that. Can I answer whether 27 million people

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:56.330
getting a raise and pulling about 2 million
people out of poverty, is that a valuable

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:02.800
trade off? Is that a fair trade off for 1.3
million not having jobs in 2025 who otherwise

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.050
would know? I can't really tell you that.
I mean, each of us has different priorities.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:12.890
What I can tell you definitively is that this
exposes the bigger truth about the minimum

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.100
wage, which I've been talking about for years
now.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:21.350
The first bigger truth is that this is really
an issue that probably has to vary state by

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.270
state. And what I mean by that is that because
the cost of living is so drastically different

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.700
in different parts of the country and even
within states, like for example in New York

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:36.000
state, which has New York City and extremely
rural low cost of living areas upstate, a

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.670
living wage in some low cost of living states
would be too low to even be a reasonable minimum

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:47.300
wage in a place that has a high cost of living
like New York City or Boston, San Francisco,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:53.890
Los Angeles. So I question even handling this
as a federal issue. Even though a seven 25

00:05:53.890 --> 00:06:00.130
federal minimum wage is outrageously low and
that needs to change. The second bigger truth

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:05.630
here is that this once again gets to what
I said to a call or last week when they said

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:09.020
does the $15 minimum wage or does is universal
basic income?

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:14.730
Does that really solve the problem that we
are dealing with? And the answer is that these

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:21.720
are not solutions to our broken economy, particularly
with automation coming 40 years of productivity

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:27.130
gains going to the richest people while the
bottom half have effectively stagnant wages,

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:32.550
increased inequality. The solution is not
going to be a $15 minimum wage. The solution

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:37.510
is not going to be universal basic income.
Now that doesn't mean don't do them. Okay,

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:43.170
but what it does mean is that we need to all
understand to really solve our problems, we've

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:47.760
got to be thinking way bigger and almost none
of our politicians are now Andrew Yang is

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.100
actually talking about this. I just don't
agree with all of his proposals. I think that

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:55.500
the VAT tax proposal he has, that's a hugely
regressive tax. I don't like that. I think

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.370
that the implementation of some of his other
ideas is the wrong one, but he is at least

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:00.700
talking about it.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.350
Last thing, really important to understand
on the backend that again, the numbers we

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:09.430
used for our discussion today might be totally
wrong because there's a university of California

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:16.460
study which says that a $15 minimum wage by
2024 simply would not cause the level of job

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:21.000
losses that the CBO study claims. So there
is a conflict there, but it doesn't matter

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.410
because none of it solves the reality that
we have a broken relationship between humans

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:31.040
and work. This includes this average 40 hour,
five day a week work week. This includes the

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.710
connection between work and health care that
we have and it includes the fact that the

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:41.180
economy values at zero a lot of work that
has value like raising kids, taking care of

00:07:41.180 --> 00:08:38.740
our elderly parents, volunteering in our communities,
and whether the minimum wage is 10 12 or $15

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:50.370
that is

00:08:50.370 --> 00:09:19.199
still going to be a problem.

